  "notes": "Oakland \"funky divas\" on Foster & McElroy's retro-soul-meets-NJS board; the research windows the entry 1990–1997 for Gate 2 and that window is used verbatim. Tempo is \"78–104 straight; ballads 63–80\", so the band runs 63–104 with the mode at 97, the sourced reading for the group's best-known uptempo, and `halfTime` stays false because the entry states straight backbeat throughout. The drum deltas are the entry's \"NJS grammar at its most literal — TR-808 snare accents, swung offbeat 16ths, R-8-style crisp hats\": swing goes to the classic MPC 0.58, the kick gains offbeat-8th share instead of the parent's tresillo lean, the snare gets an 808 stack with a hard accent tier, and the hats run continuous 16ths at a higher velocity floor than the genre. \"'Free Your Mind' swaps to a rock backbeat with straight 8ths\" is why `straightEighthsProb` is authored on the melody rather than a second mode. Harmony is \"modal minor vamps for the ballads, I–IV–V soul cadences for the uptempos; 4- and 8-bar loops\", encoded as a two-family split with the major cadence carrying real weight — unusual in this minor-leaning lane. \"Doo-wop-derived four-part block harmony\" gives the high chord-tone bias and the tight interval spread. ⚠ The INFERRED parameters are the velocity tiers, every density number and the countermelody; the research sources the NJS drum grammar at genre level, so those readings are lane inference rather than name-specific fact.",
